# Rust vs Go vs TypeScript

I'd think about them this way:

|                        |      Rust |        Go | TS/Worker |
| ---------------------- | --------: | --------: | --------: |
| Raw CPU                | **10/10** |      8/10 |    6–8/10 |
| Raw HTTP throughput    | **10/10** |      9/10 |    7–9/10 |
| Memory efficiency      | **10/10** |      8/10 |      7/10 |
| Latency predictability | **10/10** |      8/10 |      8/10 |
| Development speed      |      6/10 |  **9/10** | **10/10** |
| Edge ecosystem         |      7/10 |      5/10 | **10/10** |
| Browser shared code    |      WASM |      WASM | **10/10** |
| Agent API service      | **10/10** | **10/10** |      9/10 |
| Heavy compute          | **10/10** |      8/10 |      6/10 |
| I/O microservice       |      9/10 | **10/10** | **10/10** |

For a **traditional central API**, Go is arguably the killer compromise.

For a **maximum-performance core**, Rust.

For a **globally distributed web product**, TypeScript Workers often win overall because geography and startup behavior outweigh raw instruction throughput.

---

# What I would NOT use if raw speed is the priority

I wouldn't default new utility products to:

```text
Next.js SSR everything
+
large React hydration
+
serverless function
+
ORM
+
remote DB
+
LLM
+
three internal APIs
```

for every click.

That architecture can accidentally create:

```text
browser → edge
edge → app server
app → auth
app → DB
app → LLM
app → other API
app → DB again
server renders HTML
browser downloads React
browser hydrates React
```

A tiny application suddenly involves a pile of latency.

Instead:

```text
cached static page
+
tiny browser app
+
one API call
+
parallel upstream work
+
streaming result
```

That's the architecture I'd want across your small tools.

---

# Also: minimize internal microservices

Another common mistake.

Don't turn:

```text
generator
scorer
checker
ranker
```

into four HTTP services just because they're conceptual modules.

Initially:

```text
one process
four libraries
```

is faster.

A function call is nanoseconds-ish territory.

A network service call might be milliseconds and introduces:

* serialization
* sockets
* TLS/network
* queues
* retries
* deployment complexity

Split services only when you need independent scaling/failure boundaries.

---

# API design for agents

I think this is particularly important for the kind of products you're making.

Every useful product should expose three surfaces over the **same core**:

```text
Human web interface
REST/streaming API
MCP/tool wrapper
```

Not:

```text
website business logic
API business logic
MCP business logic
```

separately.

Architecture:

```text
             core engine
            /     |      \
           /      |       \
        Web     HTTP API    MCP
```

Agents should not scrape your webpage.

They should get:

```text
GET  /v1/...
POST /v1/...
```

with:

* stable schemas
* low nesting
* small responses
* pagination/cursors
* batch endpoints
* streaming when useful
* idempotency where writes occur
* explicit errors
* timestamps
* confidence/provenance
* machine-readable limits

---

# For agents, JSON is probably still correct

You could chase MessagePack/Protobuf.

I wouldn't yet.

For most external agent APIs:

**JSON + HTTP is the performance/compatibility optimum.**

The bottleneck generally isn't parsing 3 KB of JSON.

And an agent/tool ecosystem understands JSON universally.

For huge internal traffic, then use:

```text
Protobuf
gRPC
FlatBuffers
Arrow
```

where justified.

---

# Fast responses matter more than complete responses

A pattern I'd make standard across your apps is:

```text
POST operation

0ms       request accepted
20ms      cheap cached/deterministic result
100ms     primary external result
300ms     secondary enrichment
1000ms    AI enrichment
```

Stream each stage.

Rather than:

```text
POST
...
...
...
1000ms
giant final JSON
```

This applies to:

* domain discovery
* LLM deals
* model routing
* crypto/compute intelligence
* GitHub intelligence
* security scanners
* research systems

Anything aggregating many sources should be **incremental by default**.

---

# The most important rule

If you want a house philosophy for all these products, I'd use:

> **Static-first, local-first, cache-first, batch-first, parallel-first, stream-first. Rust where CPU actually matters.**

Then separately:

> **Never spend a network round trip doing something that can safely be done in the client or current process.**

That will have a much bigger effect on perceived and measured performance than simply declaring **"all backend code must be Rust."**

For the domain tool specifically, I'd build **v1 with TypeScript on Cloudflare**, instrument every stage down to TTFA, and only move a component to Rust when profiling demonstrates it is materially consuming the latency budget. Rust is absolutely the language I'd choose for a reusable high-performance **core engine**, but not automatically for the edge glue. ([Cloudflare Docs][4])

